---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { editorialNames, editorialKinds } from '../../lib/editorial';
import { editorialRoutes } from '../../data/editorial-examples';
import '../../styles/editorial.css';
const summaries = {
  line: 'Explain a trend with direct series labels and notes anchored to observations.',
  bar: 'Compare categories with a zero-based scale, exact labels and a publication caption.',
  dumbbell: 'Compare two values for each category. Keep the endpoints and the gap in view.',
  slopegraph: 'Compare two periods, with category names and values at both ends.',
  'small-multiples': 'Give each series its own panel while preserving one shared value scale.',
};
const schema = [
  {
    '@type': 'CollectionPage',
    name: 'Editorial charts for articles and publications',
    isAccessibleForFree: true,
  },
  {
    '@type': 'ItemList',
    itemListElement: editorialKinds.map((kind, i) => ({
      '@type': 'ListItem',
      position: i + 1,
      name: editorialNames[kind],
      url: 'https://www.chartsai.com' + editorialRoutes[kind].split('?')[0],
    })),
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="Free Editorial Charts for Articles & Reports | ChartsAI"
  description="Make publication charts with clear labels, annotations and source notes. Explore free chart makers, real-data examples and SVG, PNG, PDF or HTML downloads."
  schema={schema}
  dateModified="2026-09-09"
>
  <section class="container tool-intro">
    <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/charts/">Chart makers</a><span>/</span><span
        >Editorial charts</span>
    </nav><h1>Charts that carry the story.</h1><p>
      Free chart tools for articles, newsletters and reports. Add your data, tell readers what it means, and
      publish a graphic with its source and context intact.
    </p><a class="button" href={editorialRoutes.line}>Make an annotated line chart</a>
    <a class="text-link" href="#examples">See the sourced examples</a>
  </section>
  <section class="container editorial-resource">
    <h2>Choose the question. Then the chart.</h2><p>
      The chart type should serve the comparison you want a reader to make. A long time series needs a
      different view from a two-period comparison; overlapping lines may need separate panels. These previews
      are made by the same native chart renderer used for your downloads.
    </p><div class="editorial-collection">
      {
        editorialKinds.map((kind) => (
          <article>
            <>
              <a href={editorialRoutes[kind]}>
                <>
                  <img
                    src={`/editorial/assets/${kind}.svg`}
                    width="1200"
                    height={kind === 'small-multiples' ? 1200 : 900}
                    loading="lazy"
                    alt={`${editorialNames[kind]} with sourced ${kind === 'line' ? 'NASA temperature' : 'World Bank life expectancy'} data`}
                  />
                  <h2>{editorialNames[kind]}</h2>
                </>
              </a>
              <p>{summaries[kind]}</p>
            </>
          </article>
        ))
      }
    </div>
    <h2 id="examples">Real data. Inspectable choices.</h2><p>
      Every source-based resource includes the selected values, retrieval date, original interpretation and
      reusable downloads. They are worked examples of chart construction, not a live news feed or a substitute
      for reporting.
    </p><h3><a href="/editorial-charts/life-expectancy/">One dataset, four views of life expectancy</a></h3><p
    >
      Compare endpoints, a current snapshot within the chosen period, and complete annual paths. See why a
      positive difference between 2000 and 2023 does not mean improvement in every intervening year.
    </p><h3>
      <a href="/editorial-charts/temperature-record/">Annotating the global temperature record</a>
    </h3><p>
      Plot annual anomalies against the stated 1951–1980 baseline, retain the year spacing, and attach a note
      to a measured year. Download all 66 selected annual estimates.
    </p>
    <h2>From a spreadsheet to an article</h2><p>
      Upload or paste your table and select the columns. The importer lets you inspect sheets, row ranges,
      headers and number formats; it does not expect your full workbook to match a private template. Missing
      or ambiguous selected values are reported before they replace the chart.
    </p><p>
      Next add a headline, unit, caption, source credit, source link and data period. Optional numbered
      annotations refer to exact rows and series. The same notes appear below the plot in downloaded graphics.
      Direct labels and the visible data table help readers identify the values without relying solely on
      color or hover.
    </p><h2>Publish without an account</h2><p>
      Download a vector SVG, PNG, PDF, source CSV or editable project. A self-contained HTML figure includes
      the chart and accessible data table without external scripts, analytics or a hosted ChartsAI dependency.
      Upload it to your own site when you choose. Your private data is not placed in a public link or sent to
      another service.
    </p><p>
      For animation, the line workflow can export a compatible project for the <a
        href="https://animcharts.com/editorial-lab/">AnimCharts editorial lab</a
      >, subject to its smaller dated-series limits. You choose when to open that downloaded file there. Read
      the <a href="/editorial-charts/publishing-guide/">publishing guide</a> for formats, accessibility, revisions
      and reuse.
    </p><p>
      Made by <a href="https://www.supamakers.com/">SupaMakers</a>. All tools remain free. No forced watermark
      or ChartsAI credit; retain any attribution required by the data you use. <a href="/source/"
        >Public source and licenses</a
      >.
    </p>
  </section>
</Layout>
